workedExamples.solution_text:
Two convenient points on the tangent are $(1.5,6)$ and $(4.5,2)$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=10mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{tangentorange}{HTML}{EF8B2C}
\draw[xstep=10mm,ystep=8mm,draw={rgb,255:red,204;green,204;blue,204},thin] (0,0) grid (6,7);
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(6.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(0,7.5) node[above] {$f$};
\foreach \v in {1,...,6} {\draw[black] (\v,0.08)--(\v,-0.08);\path (\v,0) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\foreach \v in {1,...,7} {\draw[black] (0.08,\v)--(-0.08,\v);\path (0,\v) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\draw[curveblue,thick,domain=1.3:5.8,samples=80,variable=\x] plot (\x,{0.25*(\x-3)*(\x-3)-(4/3)*(\x-3)+4});
\draw[tangentorange,thick] (0.75,7)--(6,0);
\fill[black] (3,4) circle (2pt) node[above right] {$P$};
\fill[black] (1.5,6) circle (2pt) node[above right] {$(1.5,6)$};
\fill[black] (4.5,2) circle (2pt) node[below left] {$(4.5,2)$};
\draw[black,dashed] (1.5,6)--(1.5,2)--(4.5,2);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The instantaneous rate is the tangent gradient.

$\begin{aligned}f'(3)&\approx\frac{2-6}{4.5-1.5}\\&=-\frac{4}{3}\\&\approx-1.33\end{aligned}$

The negative gradient means $f$ is decreasing at $x=3$.

workedExamples.question_text:
A bowl of soup cools towards room temperature. Its temperature $T$, in degrees Celsius, is shown against time $t$, in minutes. Describe how the instantaneous rate of temperature change varies over time.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=12mm,y=10mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(5.5,0) node[right] {$t$ (min)};
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(0,5) node[above] {$T$ (${}^\circ\mathrm C$)};
\draw[black,dashed] (0,1)--(5,1);
\path (2.5,1) node[below] {room temperature};
\draw[curveblue,thick,domain=0:5,samples=80,variable=\x] plot (\x,{1+3.5*exp(-0.7*\x)});
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
The temperature is decreasing, so the tangent gradients are negative. The tangents become less steep over time, so the instantaneous rate approaches zero from below. The soup cools more slowly as it approaches room temperature.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=12mm,y=10mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{tangentorange}{HTML}{EF8B2C}
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(5.5,0) node[right] {$t$ (min)};
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(0,5) node[above] {$T$ (${}^\circ\mathrm C$)};
\draw[black,dashed] (0,1)--(5,1);
\path (2.5,1) node[below] {room temperature};
\draw[curveblue,thick,domain=0:5,samples=80,variable=\x] plot (\x,{1+3.5*exp(-0.7*\x)});
\draw[tangentorange,thick,domain=0:1.6,variable=\x] plot (\x,{1+3.5*exp(-0.56)-2.45*exp(-0.56)*(\x-0.8)});
\draw[tangentorange,thick,domain=2.5:5,variable=\x] plot (\x,{1+3.5*exp(-2.66)-2.45*exp(-2.66)*(\x-3.8)});
\fill[black] (0.8,{1+3.5*exp(-0.56)}) circle (2pt);
\fill[black] (3.8,{1+3.5*exp(-2.66)}) circle (2pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.question_text:
A secant through $P(1,f(1))$ and $Q(1+h,f(1+h))$ is used to approximate $f'(1)$. Assume a finite tangent gradient exists at $P$. Explain how to improve the approximation while keeping $P$ fixed.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=15mm,y=12mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{secantred}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(3.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,0)--(0,4.1) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[curveblue,thick,domain=0:3.2,samples=80,variable=\x] plot (\x,{0.3*(\x)*(\x)+0.5});
\draw[secantred,thick,dashed] (0.6,0.32)--(3.2,3.44);
\fill[black] (1,0.8) circle (2pt) node[above left] {$P$};
\fill[black] (3,3.2) circle (2pt) node[above left] {$Q$};
\draw[black,dashed] (1,0)--(1,0.8);
\draw[black,dashed] (3,0)--(3,3.2);
\path (1,0) node[below] {$1$};
\path (3,0) node[below] {$1+h$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
Choose a smaller nonzero interval width $h$, bringing $Q$ closer to $P$. As $h$ approaches zero, the secant gradient approaches the tangent gradient at $P$, giving a better approximation of $f'(1)$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=15mm,y=12mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
